<div class="book-title">Likutay Halachos — Orach Chaim</div>
<div class="halacha-title">Hilchos Netilas Yadayim L'Seudah — Betzi'as HaPas<br><span style="font-size:1rem;font-weight:400;">(Part 2: §§ 22–31)</span></div>
<div class="lm-subtitle">Based on <span class="v">"Ki Merachamam Yenahaigem"</span> <span class="sr">(<span class="lr">LM II, 7</span>)</span> — continuation.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 22 — <i>Karpas</i>: The Eating of Humility</div>

<div class="p">And this is the aspect of <span class="kc"><span class="h">karpas</span></span> — a type of vegetable that we eat then — which is the aspect of <span class="h">mochin d'katnus</span> [constricted consciousness]. It hints that even though we drank the first cup, we have not yet merited to eat the <span class="h">matzah</span>, which is the food of angels in the aspect of the <span class="h">man</span> [manna], as our Sages say. Rather, we must first remember our lowliness. For now, before the giving of the Torah, before <span class="h">is'arusa d'l'sata</span>, our eating is in the aspect of <span class="kc">animal food</span> — the aspect of <span class="v">"you shall eat the grass of the field"</span> <span class="sr">(Genesis 3:18)</span> — because [Adam] blemished through eating from the Tree of Knowledge. As our Sages say: when Hashem said to Adam HaRishon <span class="v">"you shall eat the grass of the field,"</span> he trembled — until [Hashem] said to him: <span class="v">"By the sweat of your brow you shall eat bread"</span> — hinting that through the exertions of Israel in Torah and <span class="h">mitzvos</span>, they will merit the eating of bread, which is human food.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 23 — <i>Yachatz</i>: Breaking Before Telling</div>

<div class="p">And this is the aspect of <span class="kc"><span class="h">Yachatz</span></span> — breaking the <span class="h">matzah</span> — to indicate that even though we break and divide it, it is impossible to eat immediately until we tell of the Exodus to include the worlds. And then we merit the eating of <span class="h">matzah</span>, which is the aspect of silence regarding the <span class="h">kushyos</span> — only we respond with many words of songs and praises for having taken us out of Egypt and revealed His G-dliness before our eyes. This is the aspect of <span class="h">lechem oni</span> [bread of poverty / bread upon which we answer] — as explained in the Hilchos Pesach. And see above (§§11–12) regarding <span class="h">betzi'as hapas</span> — that the main thing is to break with a <span class="h">berachah</span> properly so as to draw the illumination of <span class="h">Ratzon</span> during eating.</div>

<div class="p">But now on Pesach, which is the beginning of the drawing close — one cannot eat immediately. Therefore we break and shatter the <span class="h">matzah</span> beforehand — this is <span class="h">Yachatz</span> — and do not eat until the <span class="h">Haggadah</span> is recited: to indicate that now it is impossible to draw the <span class="h">tikkun</span> of the breaking (the illumination of <span class="h">Ratzon</span>) except through the telling of the Exodus, through which the worlds are included together.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 24 — The Last Two Cups: The Two Hands</div>

<div class="p">And afterward, one drinks two more cups — which correspond to the <span class="kc">two hands</span> in <span class="h">yam hachochmah</span>, which are the <span class="h">remazim</span> from which sustenance and eating [derive], and from which the illumination of <span class="h">Ratzon</span> [is drawn].</div>

<div class="p">And the overall structure of the four cups corresponds to the <span class="h">Malchus</span> that ascends to receive sustenance from the two hands in <span class="h">yam hachochmah</span>. This constitutes four aspects: <span class="h">Chochmah</span>, <span class="h">Malchus</span>, and the two hands — through which the illumination of <span class="h">Ratzon</span> shines in completeness. Therefore we conclude with <span class="kc"><span class="h">Nirtzah</span></span> — the aspect of the illumination of <span class="h">Ratzon</span>, in the wondrous <span class="h">tikkunim</span> that we merit through the entire Seder.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 25 — The Cups Are <i>Yirah</i></div>

<div class="p">And the cups themselves in which the wine of the four cups is drunk are the aspect of <span class="kc"><span class="h">yirah</span></span>. For <span class="h">kos</span> [cup] in <span class="h">gematria</span> equals <span class="h">Elokim</span>, which is the aspect of <span class="h">yirah</span> — <span class="v">"I fear G-d"</span> <span class="sr">(Genesis 42:18)</span>. For all the aspects mentioned above are included in <span class="h">yirah</span>, which is the aspect of <span class="h">Malchus</span>, as explained in the Torah teaching. Therefore the drinking of wine is called by the name of the <span class="kc">cups</span> — for the wine, which is the drawing of <span class="h">Ratzon</span>, cannot be drawn and received except through <span class="h">yirah</span>, the <span class="h">Malchus</span> that ascends and receives sustenance from <span class="h">yam hachochmah</span>. And all the illumination of son and student is likewise in the aspect of <span class="h">yirah</span>, as explained there.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 26 — The Two Washings: <i>Bitachon</i></div>

<div class="p">And this is the aspect of the <span class="kc">two washings</span> on the night of Pesach. <span class="h">Rechitzah</span> [washing] is the aspect of <span class="h">bitachon</span> [trust], for <span class="h">bitachon</span> in the Targum's language is <span class="h">rechitzah</span>. Before eating, one must wash his hands in water — this is the <span class="h">mitzvah</span> of <span class="h">netilas yadayim</span> for a meal — to purify the hands, which are "busy" [in the affairs of the world], so that one can draw the flow of sustenance from the hands in <span class="h">yam hachochmah</span>. All of this is drawn through <span class="h">bitachon</span>, as explained in the Torah teaching: all look expectantly for sustenance, and the <span class="h">Malchus</span> collects all these trusts and ascends with them to receive sustenance from the hands in <span class="h">yam hachochmah</span>.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore one needs <span class="h">rechitzah</span> in water before eating — for the water, which is the <span class="h">da'as</span> of <span class="h">yam hachochmah</span>, purifies from all impurities that come through the <span class="h">ruach shtus</span> [spirit of folly], the opposite of <span class="h">da'as</span>. And for the most part, the <span class="h">ruach shtus</span> prevents one from serving Hashem through the <span class="kc">anxiety of livelihood</span> — the <span class="h">ruach shtus</span> deceives him as if it is in the power of his hands to draw sustenance, which is the blemish of: <span class="v">"And you say: my power and the might of my hand made me this wealth"</span> <span class="sr">(Deuteronomy 8:17)</span>. Therefore one washes his hands in water — to indicate that sustenance comes only through purifying the hands in water until they are included in the hands of <span class="h">yam hachochmah</span>, from which all sustenance derives. For the hands in holiness are the aspect of <span class="h">bitachon</span> — raising one's hands to Hashem in the aspect of: <span class="v">"I spread out my hands to You; I stretch out my palms to You"</span> <span class="sr">(Psalms 143:6, 88:10)</span>. For I have no trust in "my power and the might of my hand" — only in You alone do I trust.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore it is called <span class="h">rechitzah</span> = <span class="h">bitachon</span>. For all sustenance and wealth are drawn through water, which grows all things — even treasures — in the aspect of: <span class="v">"Hashem will open for you His treasure… to give the rain of your land in its time"</span> <span class="sr">(Deuteronomy 28:12)</span>. Everything is drawn from <span class="h">yam hachochmah</span>, through the trusts of Israel.</div>

<div class="p">And now on Pesach, <span class="kc">two washings</span> are needed. Before eating the <span class="h">karpas</span>, one also washes his hands — this is the Seder sign <span class="h">U'rchatz</span>. To say: even this eating of a little vegetable, I do not have the power to draw except through trust in Your kindness alone. This is the primary completeness of <span class="h">bitachon</span>: even when a person is in great poverty and hardship, with very meager sustenance, having nothing but a little vegetable — let him not question [Hashem's] ways, but say: <span class="kc">even this little vegetable is a wondrous kindness from Hashem</span>. Therefore I trust in Him. For the G-d who sustains me with this little vegetable can sustain me with honor and great wealth as He wishes. For <span class="v">"not from the east nor from the west… for G-d judges — He humbles this one and lifts that one"</span> <span class="sr">(Psalms 75:7-8)</span>.</div>

<div class="p">And there is a hint as to why our Sages called it <span class="kc"><span class="h">karpas</span></span> — all the commentators wonder about this. But according to the above: now, at the Exodus, at the beginning of the redemption, Israel was exceedingly strong in <span class="h">bitachon</span>, as it is written: <span class="v">"and also provisions they did not make for themselves"</span> <span class="sr">(Exodus 12:39)</span>. And Scripture praises them greatly for this: <span class="v">"I remember for you the kindness of your youth… your going after Me in the wilderness"</span> <span class="sr">(Jeremiah 2:2)</span>. Therefore we draw <span class="h">bitachon</span> upon ourselves through this. For we believe that even this little vegetable is all through wondrous <span class="h">hashgachah</span> from Hashem. Therefore it is called <span class="h">karpas</span> — which is the name of <span class="kc">precious silk garments</span>, as it is written: <span class="v">"white cotton [<span class="h">chur</span>], <span class="h">karpas</span>, and blue"</span> <span class="sr">(Esther 1:6)</span>. As if to say: I trust that everything is in Your hands, and this little vegetable is a wondrous novelty like the precious garments called <span class="h">karpas</span>. And I trust in You that You can raise me up to sustain me with wealth and honor, with garments of <span class="h">karpas</span> — for everything is in Your hands alone.</div>

<div class="p">And this is the primary sweetening of <span class="h">mochin d'katnus</span>. And this is the primary <span class="h">tikkun</span> for poverty — for which the <span class="h">matzah</span> is called <span class="h">lechem oni</span> [bread of poverty]: it is a <span class="h">tikkun</span> for the poor person. For the main thing is through <span class="h">bitachon</span>, the aspect of <span class="h">matzah</span> baked in haste, for <span class="v">"they were driven from Egypt… and also provisions they did not make for themselves."</span> And this is also the eating of the <span class="h">karpas</span>: one washes his hands and eats it before all the other foods — to strengthen <span class="h">bitachon</span> from the ultimate depth of poverty, to trust in His salvation. For everything is from Him alone, and <span class="v">"the wealth and the honor are from before Him"</span> <span class="sr">(I Chronicles 29:12)</span>. Through this, the <span class="h">ruach shtus</span> is driven away — the aspect of <span class="v">"the people went out and gathered"</span> — <span class="h">b'shtusa</span> [in folly]. And one merits the <span class="h">da'as</span> mentioned above — the inclusiveness of the worlds — the aspect of the <span class="kc">turning wheel</span> explained in the Torah teaching on <span class="v">"You redeemed the tribe"</span> — the aspect of <span class="v">"He humbles this one and lifts that one"</span>, <span class="v">"He raises the needy from the dust, from the trash heap He lifts the destitute"</span> <span class="sr">(Psalms 113:5-7)</span>, <span class="v">"Hashem impoverishes and enriches, He humbles and also lifts up"</span> <span class="sr">(I Samuel 2:7)</span>. All in the aspect of the turning wheel, through which one merits the illumination of <span class="h">Ratzon</span> during eating.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 27 — Doubling Everything on Pesach Night</div>

<div class="p">It emerges that on the night of Pesach, we make <span class="kc">a change in all the above matters, doubling them</span> — and all for the sake of the illumination of <span class="h">Ratzon</span> that is drawn specifically during eating, which is the essential <span class="h">tikkun</span>, the aspect of <span class="v">"You give them their food in its time"</span> <span class="sr">(Psalms 145:15)</span>, the aspect of <span class="v">"at the [appointed] time it shall be said to Yaakov and Yisrael: what has G-d wrought!"</span> <span class="sr">(Numbers 23:23)</span> — which is the essential hope and purpose.</div>

<div class="p">For throughout the year, we draw the illumination of <span class="h">Ratzon</span> at the entrance of Shabbos through the cup of <span class="h">Kiddush</span> — for the main thing is Shabbos, the aspect of the World to Come, where the essential aspect of "the time when it shall be said… <span class="h">mah</span>…" shines — the illumination of <span class="h">Ratzon</span>. From there, all six weekdays receive, to merit the illumination of <span class="h">Ratzon</span> during eating always. And it is all according to what one merited through his service in the six weekdays — the aspect of this world — to include the worlds. In proportion, one merits on Shabbos through the <span class="h">Kiddush</span> the illumination of <span class="h">Ratzon</span>, in the aspect of: <span class="mx">"whoever labored on the eve of Shabbos shall eat on Shabbos."</span></div>

<div class="p">But now on Pesach, at its entrance on the first night, we have no service [of our own] — only His kindness. Therefore it is impossible to merit the illumination of <span class="h">Ratzon</span> through one cup of <span class="h">Kiddush</span> alone — only through reciting the <span class="h">Haggadah</span> over the second cup, telling the mighty miracles He performed for us in His great mercy, illuminating our eyes through Moshe Rabbainu and informing us of the truth of His G-dliness and His great dominion — for <span class="v">"in the beginning our fathers served idols, and now He has brought us close to His service."</span> All of this is the illumination of <span class="h">Atzilus</span> — the pinnacle of the upper world — into <span class="h">Asiyah</span>, the lower world. Only then do we merit eating the <span class="h">matzah</span> and drawing the sublime illumination of <span class="h">Ratzon</span> through the <span class="h">mitzvah</span> of eating <span class="h">matzah</span>.</div>

<div class="p">For now one must speak about this at great length and multiply the telling of the miracles of the Exodus, in order to draw the illumination of <span class="h">Ratzon</span> — since we do not yet have service and good deeds [of our own]. The primary <span class="h">tikkun</span> now is through speech — <span class="h">Pesach</span> = <span class="h">peh sach</span> [the mouth speaks] — <span class="mx">"whoever elaborates in telling is praiseworthy."</span> And similarly the washings (= trusts) are doubled, and the breakings are doubled: first before the <span class="h">Haggadah</span> [= <span class="h">Yachatz</span>] and then again at <span class="h">HaMotzi</span>. Between the two cups, two breakings, and two washings, we recite the <span class="h">Haggadah</span> and elaborate — for now the illumination of <span class="h">Ratzon</span> during eating is impossible except through this order.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 28 — For Every Person, in Every Situation</div>

<div class="p">And so it is for <span class="kc">every person</span>: as long as one has not merited through his deeds to include the worlds and reach the ultimate purpose — the illumination of <span class="h">Ratzon</span> — he must at the very least <span class="kc">multiply greatly the telling of the miracles and wonders</span> Hashem has done for him, to draw him close from wherever he is and to inform him that Hashem is still with him. All of this is the aspect of telling the Exodus, for every person.</div>

<div class="p">For through this specifically, one merits the illumination of <span class="h">Ratzon</span> during eating. The essential <span class="h">tikkun</span> is through speech, the aspect of <span class="v">"my mouth shall speak wisdoms"</span> <span class="sr">(Psalms 49:4)</span>. And whoever elaborates in this telling is praiseworthy — with <span class="h">chasadim</span> — the aspect of kindness and love and <span class="h">Ratzon</span>, the illumination of <span class="h">Ratzon</span>.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 29 — <i>Sefiras HaOmer</i>: Drawing the <i>Makifin</i></div>

<div class="p">And this is the aspect of <span class="kc"><span class="h">Sefiras HaOmer</span></span>. For it is explained in the <span class="h">Kavanos</span> that through the counting, we draw the <span class="h">makifin</span> [surrounding lights]. This is the aspect of <span class="v">"in the eyes of the nations"</span> mentioned above. For the <span class="h">makifin</span> are the intellect that is <span class="kc">impossible to grasp</span>. If so, what does their "drawing" mean? The main thing: we draw upon ourselves the surrounding intellect so that it should be <span class="kc">close to us and surround our minds</span> — meaning, that we merit to see and understand that <span class="kc">it is impossible for us to understand</span>. That we perceive from afar that there are awesome secrets with the great <span class="h">tzadikim</span>, and we are obligated to pursue them with all our might and cleave to them. Yet the secrets remain hidden from us — this is the aspect of <span class="v">"in the eyes of the nations."</span></div>

<div class="p">And this applies at every level, even at the very lowest rung. For <span class="v">"the whole earth is full of His glory."</span> Yet this is not the ultimate knowledge "that we do not know" — for we are still very, very far from the ultimate realization that we know nothing at all. Only through our drawing close to the <span class="h">tzadikim</span>, they illuminate within us that Hashem is still with us, however we are. And they inform us from afar that <span class="kc">the secret is with them</span>. This is the illumination of the <span class="h">makifin</span> for every member of Israel, in every <span class="h">midah</span> [attribute] of the seven days of building — the seven <span class="h">midos</span>.</div>

<div class="p">For the inner light is impossible to grasp due to the destruction of the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span>, since we do not merit to offer the <span class="h">Omer</span>. Only through praying during the counting — <span class="mx">"May the Merciful One restore the service of the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span> to its place"</span> — through this we draw some of the inner lights, as explained in the <span class="h">Kavanos</span>. For the offering of the <span class="h">Omer</span> of barley and its waving is the essential aspect of "the lower above" that illuminates through the sacrifices. Now during the destruction, it is impossible to illuminate the <span class="h">da'as</span> in completeness. Only through prayer and the power of the <span class="h">tzadikim</span>, we draw upon ourselves intellect and <span class="h">da'as</span> — that we should know there is a great light surrounding us, awesome intellects and secrets before our eyes, only we cannot grasp them for they are <span class="v">"too wondrous for us."</span> This is the illumination of the <span class="h">makifin</span>. And their primary illumination is that they illuminate in our <span class="h">da'as</span> to make a <span class="kc">partition and boundary in the mind</span> — not to rush into <span class="h">kushyos</span> and <span class="h">terutzim</span> that are for us in the aspect of "above time."</div>

<div class="sn">§ 30 — The <i>Makifin</i> as Cycles of Time</div>

<div class="p">And the overall <span class="h">makifin</span> are the aspect of <span class="kc"><span class="h">tekufos hayamim</span></span> [cycles of the days]. For Nissan is the aspect of <span class="h">tekufos hayamim</span>, the aspect of <span class="v">"and it was at the cycle of the days"</span> <span class="sr">(I Samuel 1:20)</span>. And Tishrei is the aspect of <span class="h">tekufas hashanah</span> [cycle of the year], said regarding <span class="h">Chag HaAsif</span> [the Ingathering Festival], which is in Tishrei. And every single day — which is the [dimension of] time — one must draw from above time, from the aspect of <span class="h">tekufos hayamim</span>, from where the illumination of the <span class="h">makifin</span> [originates]. This is the aspect of the counting [of the <span class="h">Omer</span>].</div>

<div class="sn">§ 31 — "<i>Temimos Tihyenah</i>": Wholeness Through Simplicity</div>

<div class="p"><span class="v">"And you shall count for yourselves"</span> — <span class="v">"for yourselves"</span> meaning: for each person according to his level, he must draw illumination from <span class="h">tekufos hayamim</span> — from above time — into each and every day of time, which is the illumination of the <span class="h">makifin</span>. And this is: <span class="v">"seven complete [<span class="h">temimos</span>] weeks shall they be"</span> <span class="sr">(Leviticus 23:15)</span>. For the main thing is <span class="kc"><span class="h">temimus</span> [wholeness/simplicity]</span> — not to enter into investigations and <span class="h">kushyos</span>. This is the aspect of the <span class="h">ben tam</span> [the simple son], the aspect of <span class="v">"Yaakov was a simple [<span class="h">tam</span>] man"</span> — as explained in the Torah teaching <span class="h">Mishrah</span> (<span class="lr">LM I, 30</span>).</div>

<div class="p">For the primary completeness of the worlds is when they are included one within the other — in the aspect mentioned above — which was done essentially in the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span> through the sacrifices: the upper below and the lower above. And the main thing depends on the <span class="kc">lower ones</span>: when the true <span class="h">tzadik</span> — the aspect of Moshe — informs those below, even those in the very lowest depths, that Hashem is still with them and near them, that <span class="v">"the whole earth is full of His glory"</span> — until they accept this <span class="h">da'as</span> and are aroused to draw close to Him through prayer, Torah, and good deeds, in the aspect of <span class="v">"awaken and sing, dwellers of the dust"</span> — through this, the lower are raised above. And through this, the upper are drawn below — Hashem's <span class="h">Shechinah</span> is drawn down — for <span class="mx">"through the arousal from below, there is an arousal above."</span></div>

<div class="p">This is the ultimate completeness of man and all the worlds. And its essence was in the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span>. Since it was destroyed, the luminaries have darkened, and we cannot receive any light to arouse and awaken us except through the great <span class="h">tzadikim</span> who have great <span class="h">da'as</span> that is considered as the building of the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span>, as our Sages say. They illuminate all of this within us, as explained in the Torah teaching.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore, from Pesach until <span class="h">Shavuos</span>, when we must prepare ourselves for receiving the Torah through some <span class="h">is'arusa d'l'sata</span>, the Torah commands us to occupy ourselves with the <span class="h">tikkun</span> of the <span class="h">Omer</span> of barley. Even though we have no <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span>, through prayer we draw the illumination of the <span class="h">Omer</span> offering. For the offering of the <span class="h">Omer</span> of barley and its waving is the essential aspect of <span class="kc">"the lower above"</span> — illuminated through the sacrifices.</div>

<div class="p">Now, at the beginning of the drawing close, it is a greater wonder: He commanded us to offer <span class="kc">animal food</span> — the <span class="h">Omer</span> of barley — which is the <span class="h">minchas k'na'os</span> [jealousy-offering] that eliminates the <span class="h">ruach shtus</span>, in the aspect of: <span class="v">"if a man's wife goes astray"</span> <span class="sr">(Numbers 5:12)</span>. For through the great power of the <span class="h">tzadik</span>, even this absence of <span class="h">da'as</span> — barley, animal food, the intensity of <span class="h">gevuros</span> [harsh judgments] contained in the word <span class="h">se'orah</span> [barley] (as explained in the <span class="h">Kavanos</span>) — is elevated and waved as a pleasing fragrance to Hashem. Through this specifically is the aspect of "the lower above."</div>

<div class="p">And we count afterward seven weeks until on the fiftieth day, on <span class="h">Shavuos</span>, we merit the aspect of <span class="kc">"the upper below"</span> — the aspect of <span class="v">"and Hashem descended upon Mount Sinai"</span> <span class="sr">(Exodus 19:20)</span>, drawing His <span class="h">Shechinah</span> below. This is the essential <span class="h">tikkun</span> of <span class="h">Matan Torah</span> — the aspect of <span class="h">shalom</span> [peace], making peace and completeness in all the worlds: the lower above and the upper below included together.</div>

<div class="p">And this is <span class="v">"<span class="h">temimos</span> shall they be"</span> — for the primary <span class="h">temimus</span> [wholeness/completeness] of the days and weeks within time is when they are elevated above to the aspect of "above time," to <span class="h">tekufos hayamim</span> — through knowing and believing that Hashem is still with us in every place to which we have been driven through our sins, for <span class="v">"the whole earth is full of His glory."</span> This is the essential <span class="h">da'as</span> the <span class="h">tzadik</span> illuminates within us — the aspect of Moshe — which is the aspect of the Exodus, the counting, and <span class="h">Matan Torah</span>: when Israel draws close to Hashem through the illumination of this <span class="h">da'as</span>.</div>

<div class="p">For it is explained in the Torah teaching that the essential completeness is when the two worlds are joined together — and for this one must leave behind <span class="kc">children and students</span>. For when one is above alone, that is not completeness. And this is <span class="v">"<span class="h">temimos</span> shall they be"</span> — <span class="h">temimos</span> meaning completeness: through the counting of the <span class="h">Omer</span>, which joins and includes the upper and lower together — through this is the essential <span class="h">temimus</span> and completeness.</div>

<!-- Translator's Summary -->
<div class="ts">
  <div class="sh">✦ Translator's Summary ✦</div>

  <p style="margin-bottom:.8rem;"><strong>§22 (<em>Karpas</em>)</strong>: Eating a vegetable = <em>mochin d'katnus</em>, the humility of recognizing our eating is still at the level of "animal food" before we merit the <em>matzah</em> (= manna = angelic food).</p>

  <p style="margin-bottom:.8rem;"><strong>§23 (<em>Yachatz</em>)</strong>: Breaking the <em>matzah</em> before the <em>Haggadah</em> demonstrates that the <em>tikkun</em> of breaking (= drawing <em>Ratzon</em>) cannot happen yet — only after telling of the Exodus and including the worlds.</p>

  <p style="margin-bottom:.8rem;"><strong>§§24–25</strong>: The last two cups = the two hands in <em>yam hachochmah</em>. The four cups overall = <em>Chochmah</em>, <em>Malchus</em>, and two hands → concluding with <em>Nirtzah</em> (= <em>Ratzon</em>). The cups themselves (not the wine) = <em>yirah</em>, since <em>kos</em> = <em>Elokim</em> in <em>gematria</em>: <em>Ratzon</em> can only be received through <em>yirah</em>.</p>

  <p style="margin-bottom:.8rem;"><strong>§26 (<em>Urchatz / Rachtzah</em>)</strong>: Washing = <em>bitachon</em> (trust). Water purifies from <em>ruach shtus</em>, especially the delusion of "my power made me this wealth." On Pesach: two washings — the first (before <em>karpas</em>) = trust even from the very depths of poverty: "even this little vegetable is a wondrous kindness." <em>Karpas</em> is named after precious silk garments to hint: this humble vegetable is as precious as <em>karpas</em>-silk, and I trust Hashem can raise me to that level.</p>

  <p style="margin-bottom:.8rem;"><strong>§27</strong>: Everything on the Seder night is doubled — cups, washings, breakings — because without <em>is'arusa d'l'sata</em>, the illumination of <em>Ratzon</em> requires the telling of the Exodus between the doubled acts. <em>Pesach</em> = <em>peh sach</em> (the mouth speaks).</p>

  <p style="margin-bottom:.8rem;"><strong>§28</strong>: This applies to every person: whoever hasn't yet merited the ultimate <em>tikkun</em> must at minimum multiply the telling of Hashem's miracles in his own life — his personal "Exodus."</p>

  <p style="margin-bottom:.8rem;"><strong>§§29–31 (<em>Sefiras HaOmer</em>)</strong>: The counting draws the <em>makifin</em> (surrounding lights) — intellect we cannot grasp but know exists, knowing that the secret is with the <em>tzadikim</em>. The <em>makifin</em> create a boundary in the mind against entering <em>kushyos</em>. The <em>Omer</em> of barley (= animal food) is elevated through the <em>tzadik</em>'s power = "the lower above." Seven weeks of counting → <em>Shavuos</em> = "Hashem descended on Sinai" = "the upper below." <em>Temimos tihyenah</em> = simplicity and completeness: not investigating, believing Hashem is with us, joining upper and lower through children and students.</p>
</div>


